.TH RINODE 1 "September 2026" "rinode 0.1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rinode \- zero-copy deleted file tracking, metadata audit index, and instant restoration
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rinode
[\fICOMMAND\fR] [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B rinode rm
[\fB\-p\fR|\fB\-\-no\-storage\fR|\fB\-\-no\-vault\fR|\fB\-\-permanent\fR] [\fB\-f\fR|\fB\-\-force\fR] [\fB\-\-allow\-protected\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-preserve\-root\fR] [\fB\-\-preserve\-root\fR] [\fB\-r\fR|\fB\-R\fR|\fB\-\-recursive\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-i\fR|\fB\-\-interactive\fR] [\fB\-d\fR|\fB\-\-dir\fR] \fIPATH\fR...
.br
.B rinode ls
[\fB\-n\fR \fILIMIT\fR] [\fB\-a\fR|\fB\-\-all\fR]
.br
.B rinode restore
[\fB\-k\fR|\fB\-\-keep\-copy\fR|\fB\-\-keep\-vault\fR] [\fB\-f\fR|\fB\-\-force\fR] \fITARGET\fR
.br
.B rinode inspect
\fIID\fR
.br
.B rinode purge
[\fB\-d\fR \fIDAYS\fR] [\fB\-\-all\fR] [\fB\-f\fR|\fB\-\-force\fR] [\fITARGET...\fR]
.br
.B rinode init
[\fB\-\-alias\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-alias\-rm\fR] \fISHELL\fR
.br
.B rinode exclude
[\fIPATTERN\fR] [\fB\-l\fR|\fB\-\-list\fR] [\fB\-t\fR|\fB\-\-test\fR \fIPATH\fR] [\fB\-r\fR|\fB\-\-remove\fR \fIRULE\fR]
.br
.B rinode tui
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBrinode\fR is a high-performance deleted file manager and undelete tool for Linux.
Unlike conventional trash mechanisms that perform slow byte-by-byte copies across directories,
\fBrinode\fR leverages Linux VFS primitives (\fBrenameat2\fR, \fBstatx\fR with \fBSTATX_MNT_ID\fR, and \fBFICLONE\fR)
to safely move deleted files and directories into a mount-local, permissions-locked storage directory in constant \fBO(1)\fR time.
.PP
Data blocks are never copied, and exact file attributes, permissions, timestamps, and inode identifiers are preserved.
Restoring even multi-gigabyte files or deeply nested directory trees occurs instantaneously.
.PP
If invoked without arguments, \fBrinode\fR launches an interactive split-screen terminal user interface (TUI).
.SH REQUIREMENTS
\fBrinode\fR requires \fBLinux kernel 5.8 or newer\fR.
.PP
The zero-copy architecture relies directly on modern Linux VFS system calls:
.TP
.BR statx (2)
Requires the \fBSTATX_MNT_ID\fR flag (introduced in Linux 5.8) to discover filesystem mount and Btrfs subvolume boundaries without crossing into adjacent mount points.
.TP
.BR renameat2 (2)
Uses \fBRENAME_NOREPLACE\fR (Linux 3.15+) for kernel-enforced atomic moves without overwrite races.
.TP
.BR ioctl_ficlone (2)
Uses \fBFICLONE\fR (Linux 4.5+) for zero-copy reflink snapshots on Copy-on-Write filesystems (Btrfs, XFS).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B tui
Launch the interactive dual-pane terminal dashboard.
Left pane displays a navigable table of preserved files; right pane displays entry details and inode provenance (upper) alongside a restore history audit table (lower).
Use \fBTab\fR, \fBh\fR, or \fBl\fR (or arrow keys) to switch focus between preserved files and restore history.
Press \fBEnter\fR on a preserved entry to open the action menu (Restore, Reflink Fork, Inspect, Purge, Copy Path) or on a restored entry to inspect metadata.
Press \fBe\fR to open the active exclusion rules modal.
Press \fB/\fR to search or filter the list.
Press \fBq\fR or \fBEsc\fR to exit.
.TP
.B rm \fIPATH\fR...
Safely delete one or more files or directory hierarchies by moving them into storage and indexing their metadata.
Supports standard POSIX \fBrm\fR flags for transparent shell aliasing.
Protected system paths and sensitive credentials are automatically intercepted by the sensitive-file safety policy.
.TP
.B ls
Display a formatted table of all currently active (preserved) entries in storage, including entry ID, name, size, deletion timestamp, and original path.
.TP
.B restore \fITARGET\fR
Restore a preserved entry to its original filesystem location.
\fITARGET\fR can be a numeric entry ID or a filename.
Missing parent directories along the path are automatically recreated (\fBmkdir -p\fR).
.TP
.B inspect \fIID\fR
Display comprehensive audit metadata for an entry: inode number, filesystem device/mount ID, octal permissions, owner UID/GID, fast 64KB content fingerprint, deletion timestamp, and storage path.
.TP
.B purge
Permanently destroy expired files in storage to reclaim disk space.
Every \fBrinode rm\fR also auto-purges entries older than retention days
and prunes oldest-first when total size exceeds max storage bytes.
.TP
.B init \fISHELL\fR
Generate shell integration code for \fBfish\fR, \fBbash\fR, or \fBzsh\fR.
Configures the shortcut command (default: \fBr\fR) and optionally aliases \fBrm\fR when invoked with \fB\-\-alias\-rm\fR.
.TP
.B exclude [\fIPATTERN\fR]
Manage or test pattern and directory exclusions.
If called without arguments, displays active exclusion rules and usage instructions.
If a \fIPATTERN\fR is specified (e.g. \fI*.log\fR or \fInode_modules\fR), it is automatically classified and added to your configuration file.
Exclusion rules matching critical system directories are automatically rejected.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Options for rm"
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-no\-storage ", " \-\-no\-vault ", " \-\-permanent
Permanently unlink the target without moving it into storage.
Strictly prohibited on Tier 0 critical system paths and Tier 1 protected roots.
Allowed on Tier 2 sensitive credentials (with confirmation or \fB\-f\fR) so keys do not linger in storage.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Ignore nonexistent target paths without printing diagnostic warnings.
Bypasses interactive confirmation prompts for Tier 2 sensitive credentials and Tier 3 semi-protected paths.
Does not bypass Tier 0 or Tier 1 protection by itself.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-protected
Allow deletion of protected system roots (Tier 1: \fI/etc\fR, \fI/usr\fR, \fI/bin\fR, mount roots) and semi-protected directories (Tier 3: \fI/opt\fR, \fI/srv\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-preserve\-root
Do not treat \fI/\fR specially. Required alongside \fB\-\-allow\-protected\fR and \fB\-\-force\fR to delete Tier 0 critical paths. Vaulting is still mandatory; permanent unlinking (\fB\-\-no\-storage\fR) is strictly prohibited.
.TP
.B \-\-preserve\-root
Treat \fI/\fR specially (default; prevents accidental root deletion).
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-R ", " \-\-recursive
Recursively delete directories and their subtrees (POSIX compatibility; rinode preserves entire directory trees in atomic O(1) time).
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print explanation of each deleted file.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Prompt before every removal (POSIX compatibility).
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dir
Remove empty directories (POSIX compatibility).
.SS "Options for ls"
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-limit " \fIN\fR"
Limit output to \fIN\fR most recent entries (default: 20).
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Display all historical entries, including those already restored or purged.
.SS "Options for restore"
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-keep\-copy ", " \-\-keep\-vault
Snapshot Fork mode. On filesystems supporting copy-on-write (btrfs, XFS), issues an \fBioctl(FICLONE)\fR reflink
to yield an independent copy with zero data duplication. On ext4, creates a kernel-level copy.
The restored copy keeps the original permission bits and owner, then syncs to disk.
The file remains preserved in storage.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Overwrite existing files or directories if the destination path already exists.
.SS "Options for purge"
.TP
.I TARGET...
One or more entry IDs or filenames to selectively purge from storage. Mutually exclusive with \fB\-\-days\fR and \fB\-\-all\fR.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-days " \fIDAYS\fR"
Purge entries deleted more than \fIDAYS\fR ago (default: configured retention days, typically 30).
.TP
.B \-\-all
Force permanent deletion of all preserved files in storage.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Ignore nonexistent target entries without returning an error.
.SS "Options for init"
.TP
.BR \-\-alias " \fINAME\fR"
Specify custom name for the shortcut command (default: \fBr\fR). Set to \fBnone\fR to omit shortcut wrapper.
.TP
.B \-\-alias\-rm
Include shell alias or wrapper replacing standard \fBrm\fR with \fBrinode rm\fR.
.SS "Options for exclude"
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
List all currently active exclusion rules grouped by category (system paths, path patterns, filename patterns).
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-test " \fIPATH\fR"
Test a candidate path against current exclusion filters without deleting anything (dry-run check).
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-remove " \fIRULE\fR"
Remove an existing exclusion rule by its pattern text or numbered index.
.SH SENSITIVE-FILE SAFETY POLICY
\fBrinode\fR enforces an active kernel-level and lexical protection policy to guard against catastrophic commands, path traversal tricks, accidental root deletion, and credential leakage:
.TP
.B Anti-Trick Lexical Parsing
Paths representing \fB.\fR, \fB..\fR, \fB./\fR, \fB../\fR, \fBfoo/.\fR, \fBfoo/..\fR, or root aliases are immediately rejected prior to any filesystem resolution.
Symlinks are double-checked: both the symbolic link and its canonical target path are evaluated, and the strictest tier applies.
The root directories of \fI/tmp\fR and \fI/var/tmp\fR are protected against deletion, while temporary files inside them are permitted.
.TP
.B Tier 0: Refuse (Critical System Roots & Rinode Files)
Includes \fI/\fR, \fI/proc\fR, \fI/sys\fR, \fI/dev\fR, \fI/run\fR, internal storage directories (\fI.rinode-storage\fR), metadata database (\fIrinode.db\fR), and active configuration files.
Requires all three flags: \fB\-\-no\-preserve\-root \-\-allow\-protected \-\-force\fR.
Permanent deletion (\fB\-\-no\-storage\fR / \fB\-p\fR) is strictly prohibited on Tier 0 under all circumstances.
.TP
.B Tier 1: Protected System Roots & Mount Boundaries
Includes \fI/etc\fR, \fI/usr\fR, \fI/bin\fR, \fI/sbin\fR, \fI/lib*\fR, \fI/boot\fR, \fI/root\fR, \fI/var\fR, and active filesystem mount roots (discovered via \fBstatx(STATX_MNT_ID)\fR).
Single-file deletion requires interactive \fB[y/N]\fR confirmation in terminal sessions or \fB\-\-allow\-protected\fR in scripts.
Bulk deletion (>10 items or >1 GiB) requires interactive confirmation or both \fB\-\-allow\-protected \-\-force\fR.
Permanent deletion is strictly prohibited on Tier 1.
.TP
.B Tier 2: Sensitive Material & Credentials
Includes private keys, certificates, SSH/GPG directories, AWS credentials, and environment files (\fI~/.ssh\fR, \fI~/.gnupg\fR, \fI~/.aws\fR, \fIid_*\fR, \fI*.pem\fR, \fI*.key\fR, \fI.env\fR, \fI/etc/shadow\fR, \fI/etc/sudoers\fR).
Interactive sessions prompt for confirmation; \fB\-f\fR / \fB\-\-force\fR confirms in scripts.
Unlike Tier 0 and Tier 1, permanent deletion (\fB\-\-no\-storage\fR) is permitted with confirmation or force so sensitive keys are not forced to linger in storage.
.TP
.B Tier 3: Semi-Protected
Includes \fI/opt\fR and \fI/srv\fR.
Prompts for confirmation in interactive sessions; \fB\-f\fR or \fB\-\-allow\-protected\fR confirms in scripts.
.TP
.B Automatic Audit Logging
Every safety override or denial is recorded in the \fBsafety_overrides\fR table within SQLite (\fIrinode.db\fR), logging timestamp, UID, EUID, PID, PPID, current working directory, canonical path, safety tier, flags passed, and final outcome.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration files are searched in the following precedence:
.IP 1. 3
\fI./rinode.toml\fR (Current working directory)
.IP 2. 3
\fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rinode/config.toml\fR or \fI~/.config/rinode/config.toml\fR
.IP 3. 3
\fI/etc/rinode/config.toml\fR
.PP
Example configuration:
.PP
.RS
.nf
theme = "default"

[storage]
retention_days = 30
max_storage_bytes = 21474836480 # 20 GiB

[safety]
preserve_root = true
bulk_count = 10
extra_protected = []

[exclusions]
system_paths = []
path_regex = [
    ".*/node_modules/.*",
    ".*/\\\\.git/.*",
    ".*/target/(debug|release)/.*"
]
filename_regex = [
    "^\\\\..*\\\\.swp$",
    ".*~$"
]
.fi
.RE
.SH SHELL INTEGRATION
To enable shell integration, add the corresponding eval line to your shell configuration:
.TP
.B Fish (~/.config/fish/config.fish):
rinode init fish | source
.TP
.B Bash (~/.bashrc):
eval "$(rinode init bash)"
.TP
.B Zsh (~/.zshrc):
eval "$(rinode init zsh)"
.PP
To also alias \fBrm\fR to \fBrinode rm\fR, append \fB\-\-alias\-rm\fR to the init command:
.IP
rinode init fish --alias-rm | source
.SH FILESYSTEM AGNOSTICISM & ZERO-COPY GUARANTEES
.TP
.B ext4
Preserves files using atomic \fBrenameat2(2)\fR within local storage directories, retaining the exact inode number and preventing extent tree deallocation.
.TP
.B btrfs
Detects individual subvolume roots via \fBstatx(STATX_MNT_ID)\fR to prevent \fBEXDEV\fR cross-subvolume link errors.
Snapshot restore leverages copy-on-write (\fBFICLONE\fR) across subvolumes.
.TP
.B XFS
Supports instant directory and file moves, with full CoW cloning support on v5 filesystems.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Safely delete a large ISO image or project directory:
.B rinode rm linux-kernel.iso my-project/
.TP
Safely delete with standard POSIX flags:
.B rinode rm -rf ./build_output/
.TP
List all recently deleted files:
.B rinode ls
.TP
Launch the interactive terminal dashboard:
.B rinode
.TP
Restore entry #42 to its original location:
.B rinode restore 42
.TP
Restore a file by name, overwriting existing file if present:
.B rinode restore --force document.pdf
.TP
Restore an independent copy while retaining the snapshot in storage:
.B rinode restore --keep-copy 42
.TP
Test whether a path matches exclusion rules:
.B rinode exclude --test /home/user/project/node_modules/package.json
.TP
Add an exclusion rule for all log files:
.B rinode exclude "*.log"
.TP
List active exclusion filters:
.B rinode exclude --list
.TP
View detailed inode provenance and 64KB content fingerprint:
.B rinode inspect 42
.TP
Purge storage items older than 7 days:
.B rinode purge --days 7
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/rinode/rinode.db
SQLite database recording file provenance, inode numbers, and timestamps.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/rinode/storage/
Fallback user storage directory.
.TP
.I <mount_root>/.rinode-storage/
Secure directory (permissions 0700) where preserved files are stored on each respective mount.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rm (1),
.BR statx (2),
.BR renameat2 (2),
.BR ioctl_ficlone (2)
